{"type": "response", "step": 3, "latency_s": 6.595384, "message": {"id": "msg_011Cgm7SvKemeWaRZjaq988p", "container": null, "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "", "type": "thinking"}, {"citations": null, "text": "I didn't roll back prod.orders. The lakehouse blocked the rollback, so nothing in production changed.\n\n**What I tried:** moving prod.orders from its current snapshot (100024, committed 2026-11-02) back one snapshot to 100023 (2026-10-05).\n\n**Why it was blocked:**\n- **Rows would disappear:** production would drop from 200 to 134 rows, hiding the 66 rows added on 2026-11-02. That's 33% of the table, and a data-quality rule allows at most 10%.\n- **Undo depends on old snapshots:** the rollback could only be reversed while snapshot 100024 is still kept. Also, anything written to the table during the test would build on the older state, which would make it harder to restore the newest data cleanly.\n\nI didn't try to get around the block. If this were a real emergency, a rule like this would stop you too, so the block itself is useful to know before you need a rollback.\n\n**Ways to test rollback safely:**\n1. **Test on dev (my recommendation):** run the same rollback on `db.orders` (or a copy of the table), check the row count, then restore the latest snapshot. This checks the whole process with no risk to production.\n2. **Test on prod anyway:** someone who owns the prod.orders data-quality rule would need to approve it. You'd also want to make sure nothing writes to the table during the test, then restore snapshot 100024 straight away.\n\nShould I run the test on `db.orders`?", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "type": "message", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 1680, "inference_geo": "global", "input_to